## Git Revert

`git revert` creates a **new commit** that reverses the changes introduced by an earlier commit.

Unlike `git reset`, it preserves the existing commit history.

```bash
git revert <commit_id>
```

### Reset vs Revert

| Feature                      | `git reset`    | `git revert` |
| ---------------------------- | -------------- | ------------ |
| Moves `HEAD`                 | Yes            | No           |
| Creates new commit           | No             | Yes          |
| Preserves existing history   | Can rewrite it | Yes          |
| Suitable for shared branches | Usually avoid  | Recommended  |

