# Git Branching Techniques

A Git branch is an independent line of development.

Branches allow developers to work on features without directly affecting the main production branch.

```text
master/main:
cm1 → cm2 → cm3 → cm4

feature:
cm1 → cm2 → cm3 → f1cm1 → f1cm2 → f1cm3
```

After development is complete, the feature branch can be merged into the target branch.

---

## Branching Strategies

### Scenario 1 — Single Developer, One Feature

```text
master:
rel1 → rel2 → rel3

feature_branch1:
rel1 → rel2 → rel3 → f1cm1 → f1cm2 → f1cm3
```

Merge:

```bash
git switch master
git merge feature_branch1
```

---

### Scenario 2 — Single Developer, Multiple Features

```text
master:
rel1 → rel2 → rel3

feature_branch1:
rel1 → rel2 → rel3 → f1cm1 → f1cm2 → f1cm3

feature_branch2:
rel1 → rel2 → rel3 → f2cm1 → f2cm2 → f2cm3
```

---

### Scenario 3 — Multiple Developers

A common structure can include:

```text
master
   |
   └── integration
          |
          ├── developer1
          │      ├── feature1
          │      └── feature2
          |
          └── developer2
                 ├── feature1
                 └── feature2
```

Developers work independently and changes are integrated through the appropriate integration branch.

---

### Scenario 4 — Multiple Teams

A larger organization may use a hierarchy such as:

```text
master
   |
   └── release
          |
          ├── integration-team1
          │      ├── developer1
          │      └── developer2
          |
          └── integration-team2
                 ├── developer1
                 └── developer2
```

The exact branching strategy should be based on the organization's development and release process.

